          <avActivityHighlights>Fresh and older wind slabs are to be assessed with care and prudence.</avActivityHighlights>
          <avActivityComment>The sometimes strong wind has transported the new snow significantly. Adjacent to ridgelines and in gullies and bowls the wind slabs have increased in size additionally. Above approximately 2300 m the avalanches can be triggered in the new snow and wind slab layers and reach large size. &lt;br/&gt; New snow and wind slabs can in some cases be released by a single winter sport participant. This applies in particular on very steep slopes at intermediate and high altitudes. &lt;br/&gt; The Avalanche Warning Service currently has only a small amount of information that has been collected in the field, so that the avalanche danger should be investigated especially thoroughly in the relevant locality.</avActivityComment>
          <snowpackStructureComment>30 to 40 cm of snow fell in the last few days above approximately 1500 m. The sometimes moderate wind has transported the new snow. Faceted weak layers exist in the snowpack in particular in places that are protected from the wind, especially in the Prealps.</snowpackStructureComment>
        </BulletinMeasurements>
      </bulletinResultsOf>
    </Bulletin>
